      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "config.Load() - Configuration Materialization \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:249\n├── parseOptions() - handle WithConfigFile \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:250\n├── userHomeDir() - get user home directory \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:252\n├── newConfiguredViper() \u003c-- 3a\n│   ├── viper.New() \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:282\n│   ├── SetEnvPrefix(\"FOXCTL\") \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:284\n│   └── AutomaticEnv() \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:286\n├── applyDefaults(v, defaultHome) \u003c-- 3b\n│   ├── SetDefault(\"home\", defaultHome) \u003c-- 3c\n│   ├── SetDefault(\"paths.*\", ...) \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:294\n│   ├── SetDefault(\"database.driver\", \"libsql\") \u003c-- 3d\n│   ├── SetDefault(\"logging.*\", ...) \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:304\n│   └── SetDefault(\"cas.*\", ...) \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:320\n├── configureConfigFile() - set config path \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:260\n├── readConfig() - v.ReadInConfig() \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:261\n│   └── if err := v.ReadInConfig() \u003c-- 3e\n├── decodeConfig(v) \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:265\n│   ├── if err := v.Unmarshal(\u0026cfg) \u003c-- 3f\n│   └── parsePluginPathList() \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:348\n└── finalizeConfig(cfg, home) \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:270\n    ├── resolvePath() for all paths \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:354\n    ├── normalize durations/defaults \u003c-- internal/platform/config/config.go:372\n    └── Apply env var overrides\n        └── TURSO_DATABASE_URL override \u003c-- 3g",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe `foxctl` CLI needs to support **multiple configuration sources** with clear precedence rules: hardcoded defaults, YAML config files, and environment variables. The challenge is that configuration must be **materialized once** at startup, combining all these layers in the correct order, while supporting both explicit config file paths (`--config`) and automatic discovery (`~/.foxctl/config.yaml`). The system must also handle **missing config files gracefully** (falling back to defaults) while still allowing env vars to override any setting [3f].\n\n## Details\n\n### Configuration Loading Pipeline\n\nThe `config.Load()` function [3a] orchestrates a **five-stage pipeline** that builds the final configuration:\n\n1. **Viper Setup** [3a]: Creates a viper instance configured with `FOXCTL_` prefix and automatic environment variable binding with dot-to-underscore conversion (e.g., `database.driver` → `FOXCTL_DATABASE_DRIVER`)\n\n2. **Apply Defaults** [3b]: Sets hardcoded defaults for all configuration fields including home directory (`~/.foxctl`) [3c], database driver (`libsql` for local-first) [3d], logging settings, and CAS policies\n\n3. **Read YAML** [3e]: Attempts to read `~/.foxctl/config.yaml` (or path from `--config` flag). If the file doesn't exist, this step is **silently skipped** - the system continues with defaults\n\n4. **Unmarshal to Struct** [3f]: Viper's merged configuration (defaults + YAML + env vars) is unmarshaled into a typed `Config` struct. At this point, **environment variables automatically override** both defaults and YAML values\n\n5. **Finalize** [3g]: Post-processing step that resolves relative paths, normalizes durations, and applies special env var overrides like `TURSO_DATABASE_URL` for compatibility with Turso's standard environment variables\n\n### Key Design Decisions\n\n**Layered precedence**: Environment variables have the **highest priority**, followed by YAML config, then hardcoded defaults. This allows production deployments to override config files via env vars without modifying files.\n\n**Graceful degradation**: Missing config files are not errors - the system falls back to sensible defaults. This makes `foxctl` work out-of-the-box without requiring configuration.\n\n**Path resolution**: All paths support `~` expansion and relative paths (resolved relative to `~/.foxctl`), making configs portable across machines."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "Context Wiring: Config + Logger Injection",
      "description": "Shows how PersistentPreRunE loads config and logger into context for downstream commands",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "4a",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go",
          "lineNumber": 39,
          "lineContent": "if _, ok := config.FromContext(cmd.Context()); ok {",
          "title": "Check if config already in context",
          "description": "Skip loading if config was already injected (e.g., by tests or parent command)"
        },
        {
          "id": "4b",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go",
          "lineNumber": 47,
          "lineContent": "cfg, err := config.Load(cmd.Context(), opts...)",
          "title": "Load configuration",
          "description": "Materializes full config from defaults, YAML, and env vars"
        },
        {
          "id": "4c",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go",
          "lineNumber": 51,
          "lineContent": "logger := logging.New(logging.Config{",
          "title": "Create logger from config",
          "description": "Builds zerolog logger with level and format from config.Logging"
        },
        {
          "id": "4d",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go",
          "lineNumber": 55,
          "lineContent": "ctx := config.WithContext(cmd.Context(), cfg)",
          "title": "Store config in context",
          "description": "Makes config available to all downstream code via context"
        },
        {
          "id": "4e",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go",
          "lineNumber": 56,
          "lineContent": "ctx = logging.WithContext(ctx, logger)",
          "title": "Store logger in context",
          "description": "Makes logger available to all downstream code via context"
        },
        {
          "id": "4f",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go",
          "lineNumber": 57,
          "lineContent": "cmd.SetContext(ctx)",
          "title": "Update command context",
          "description": "Replaces command's context with enriched version containing config and logger"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Cobra Command Execution Flow\n└── rootCmd.Execute() \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go:31\n    └── Cobra framework invokes hooks\n        ├── cobra.OnInitialize callbacks\n        │   └── initConfig() sets up viper \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go:62\n        │       └── (loads .env files - trace 2)\n        └── rootCmd.PersistentPreRunE hook \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/root.go:38\n            ├── Check if config exists \u003c-- 4a\n            ├── config.Load() \u003c-- 4b\n            │   └── (materializes config - trace 3)\n            ├── logging.New() \u003c-- 4c\n            │   └── Creates zerolog.Logger \u003c-- internal/platform/logging/logging.go:82\n            ├── config.WithContext() \u003c-- 4d\n            │   └── Stores cfg in context.Value \u003c-- internal/platform/config/context.go:9\n            ├── logging.WithContext() \u003c-- 4e\n            │   └── Stores logger in context.Value \u003c-- internal/platform/logging/logging.go:104\n            └── cmd.SetContext(ctx) \u003c-- 4f\n                └── Updates command's context",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nEvery CLI command needs access to **configuration** (database paths, logging settings, API credentials) and a **logger** before it can do useful work. Loading these dependencies naively on every command invocation would be wasteful—you'd parse YAML files, merge environment variables, and construct loggers repeatedly. Instead, foxctl uses **Cobra's lifecycle hooks** to load configuration and logger **exactly once per command execution** and inject them into a shared context that all downstream code can access [4a, 4b, 4c].\n\nThis solves two problems:\n1. **Avoid redundant initialization**: Config is loaded once in `PersistentPreRunE`, not in every subcommand\n2. **Dependency injection without globals**: Config and logger flow through `context.Context` rather than package-level variables\n\n## Details\n\n### The Hook Registration\n\nDuring CLI initialization, `rootCmd.PersistentPreRunE` is registered as a hook that runs **before every command** [4f is called from root.go:38]. This hook is the single chokepoint where configuration and logging are wired up.\n\n### The Execution Flow\n\nWhen a user runs `foxctl run my-skill`:\n\n1. **Check for existing config** [4a]: If config is already in context (e.g., from tests or parent commands), skip loading to avoid redundant work\n2. **Load configuration** [4b]: Call `config.Load()` which merges defaults, YAML files, and environment variables into a typed `Config` struct\n3. **Create logger** [4c]: Construct a `zerolog.Logger` using the log level and format from the loaded config\n4. **Inject into context** [4d, 4e]: Store both config and logger in `context.Context` using type-safe keys\n5. **Update command context** [4f]: Replace the command's context with the enriched version\n\n### Context Retrieval Pattern\n\nDownstream code retrieves dependencies using **context accessor functions**:\n- `config.MustFromContext(ctx)` extracts the config [4d stores it at context.go:9]\n- `logging.FromContext(ctx)` extracts the logger [4e stores it at logging.go:104]\n\nThis pattern ensures **no global state** and makes testing straightforward—tests can inject mock configs by calling `config.WithContext()` before executing commands.\n\n### Why PersistentPreRunE?\n\nThe \"Persistent\" prefix means this hook runs for **all subcommands** (run, daemon, jobs, etc.). The \"E\" suffix means it can return errors, allowing config loading failures to abort command execution cleanly before any business logic runs."
    },
